AB  - AMASES are a revolutionary approach to automating the Software Development Life Cycle (SDLC) in a system of intelligent agents working collaboratively. This paper will suggest AMASES as a new engineers-oriented model that consists of Requirement Engineering, Architecture Design, Code Generation, Testing, Security and Compliance, Deployment, and Monitoring Agents modulated by an Adaptive Autonomous Multi-Agents-Task Orchestration (AAMTO) algorithm. The framework combines Large Language Models (LLMs), Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG), Chain-of-Thought (CoT) reasoning, and a knowledge repository to facilitate context-driven decision-making, dynamic task allocation, inter-agent interaction, and lifelong learning through the software engineering lifecycle. The methodology proposed will assist in automated requirement analysis, software design, implementation, testing, security validation, implementation and optimization of the software running with minimum human interference. The benchmark datasets included HumanEval, MBPP, Defects4J, and PROMISE and were evaluated experimentally. The AMASES framework proposed obtained better accuracy of task completion (97.4%), accuracy of code generation (96.8%), defect detection rate (95.9%), software quality (97.2%), and deployment success rate (98.1%), than existing frameworks such as ChatDev, AgentVerse, MetaGPT, and MARE. The findings indicate that AMASES has a substantial positive impact on the quality of software, the efficiency of software development and scalability, and the autonomous decision-making, thus, becoming a promising solution to AI-driven software engineering in the next generation.
