AB  - Developing autonomous artificial intelligence agents that can reason, use tools, and coordinate multi-step workflows remains a software engineering challenge. Many existing systems use reactive loops or manually specified workflows, which can make state management, termination, and refinement difficult to inspect. This research proposes a graph-driven Meta-Agent architecture that treats agent generation as a compilation process and transforms high-level intent into structured executable graphs. The evaluated implementation combines LangGraph orchestration, Model Context Protocol (MCP) tool interfaces, Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG), and confidence-guided Critic–Refiner routing. Under a fixed language-model configuration, we evaluated the Full System and five matched controls on 240 paired software-engineering tasks. The Full System completed 220 tasks (91.7%), compared with 156 (65.0%) for a ReAct baseline, 168 (70.0%) without RAG, 188 (78.3%) without refinement, 193 (80.4%) with static rather than MCP-based tool discovery, and 200 (83.3%) without DSPy prompt optimization. Continuity-corrected McNemar tests with Holm adjustment found each paired difference significant ( $p \leq 0.0051$ ). The task-level outcomes are supplied as supplementary material. These results are limited to the evaluated task suite and fixed configuration, but provide controlled evidence for the contributions of graph orchestration, retrieval, refinement, tool discovery, and prompt optimization.
ER  - 

