AB  - Autonomous agents are becoming common in software development, but they often struggle to maintain context in large, changing codebases. To solve this, we propose CA-SAF (Codebase-Aware Self-Adapting Framework), a multi-agent system designed to fix this architectural "blindness" by actively retrieving precise code details. Our core innovation is ZSDM (Zero-Shot Dependency Mapping). This hybrid method combines AST parsing with strict LLM extraction to map every entity and relationship into a queryable Knowledge Graph. By using structured code summaries, we achieved roughly 80% data compression, ensuring the context remains efficient. We benchmarked the system against GPT-4o, LLaMA 3.1, and Claude 3 Opus. We found that Claude 3 Opus, with its 200,000 - token window, slightly outperformed the others (capped at 128,000) on complex, non-local mapping tasks. Over a six-month operational study, using CA-SAF led to a measurable drop in both Cycle Time and Lead Time for maintenance work. Crucially, the framework used this codebase awareness to balance speed with quality, actively reducing the number of critical bugs that high-speed LLM generation often introduces.
ER  - 

