AB  - As software development becomes intelligent and more autonomous, Reinforcement Learning (RL) and Agentic AI are gaining interest as a means to automate complex software development tasks. The existing studies are scattered over RL-based code generation, program repair, testing, debugging, task planning, software maintenance, and autonomous AI agents, and it is hard to have a systematic understanding of their models, applications, capabilities, and research trends. This study conducts a systematic analysis of the current and available studies on RL models and Agentic AI applications in SD, based on the RL algorithms, agent architectures, software engineering tasks, datasets, evaluation metrics, and outcomes reported. It classifies conventional RL, Deep Reinforcement Learning (DRL) and advanced policy- and value-based approaches and investigates the planning and reasoning capabilities, as well as the use of tools, memory, feedback and multi-agent collaboration by Agentic AI in software development tasks. The results have shown that the use of RL and Agentic AI can enhance the quality of code generation, automated testing, defect detection, program repair, task planning, and increase efficiency in the development process, and that agentic architectures offer more autonomy and adaptability than traditional approaches to code generation and automation. However, issues of design of rewards, computational costs, reliability, control of hallucinations, standardized benchmarks, security and reproducibility are yet to be addressed. This systematic study offers a unified research viewpoint and future research opportunities for creating reliable, explainable, secure, and autonomous software engineering systems using AI.
ER  - 

