AB  - AI coding agents—Claude Code, GitHub Copilot, OpenAI Codex, and Cursor—increasingly rely on developer-authored instruction les (CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, copilot-instructions.md, etc.) to understand project context. These agent manifests have not been systematically studied. We present the first large-scale content analysis of 80 real-world manifests drawn from five agent types using a six-category taxonomy and a three-model LLM classifier with majority-vote consensus. Workflow & Process dominates (present in 96.2% of manifests, 43.5% mean character fraction), while evolution context—information about how the software changes over time—is largely absent: 73.8% of manifests contain no evolution content, and none references Lehman’s laws of software evolution. A single significant project-level correlation emerges: manifests in more law-conformant projects contain more coding-style guidance (ρ = 0.431, p = 0.040, n=23). These findings characterise a systematic evolution context gap that leaves agents architecture-blind and motivate automated manifest enrichment tooling.
ER  - 

