AB  - Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly used as reasoning engines for autonomous agents, yet their outputs remain vulnerable to inconsistency, logical errors, hallucination, and unstable multi-step inference processes. Reflection-based reasoning has emerged as an effective inference-time mechanism for improving reliability by allowing models to critique, verify, and iteratively revise their own outputs. This paper presents a controlled cross-domain empirical evaluation of three reflection strategies: Self-Refine, Reflexion, and Self-Consistency with Verification, against a baseline single-step inference approach. Experiments use DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-1.5B across three core LLM evaluation datasets—GSM8K, HotpotQA, and MBPP—measuring accuracy, inference time, and token usage. Results show that reflection improves accuracy across all domains: Reflexion achieves the largest relative accuracy gains, with improvements of +8% on GSM8K, +4% on HotpotQA, and +6% on MBPP. However, all reflection methods introduce increased computational cost, especially in code generation tasks. The findings indicate that reflection is a context-dependent strategy whose practical value depends on task structure, accuracy requirements, and available computational resource constraints.
ER  - 

