AB  - The evolution of Large Language Models (LLMs) from specialized systems to versatile, essential platforms has created an urgent demand for thorough and effective evaluation methods. Nonetheless, current assessment methods continue to be disjointed, depending on diverse benchmarks and metrics that do not adequately reflect actual performance, reasoning accuracy, and alignment with safety standards. This document offers a cohesive and structured examination of LLM assessment via a hierarchical classification that encompasses capability dimensions, evaluation methods, and metric formulation. Expanding upon this framework, we perform an extensive meta-analysis of 312 benchmarks released since 2020, uncovering ongoing disparities in capability coverage, methodological compromises, and a notable performance gap in tasks involving autonomous agents. We present the evaluation trilemma to elucidate these limitations, highlighting the fundamental conflict between authenticity, scalability, and contamination resistance in existing evaluation frameworks. We conduct a deeper examination of innovative evaluation methods that integrate learning-based and hybrid strategies to tackle these challenges. The results underscore the necessity for cohesive and flexible assessment frameworks that accurately represent practical implementation scenarios and establish a systematic basis for progressing the evaluation of future LLMs.
ER  - 

