AB  - Talk shapes code now, not strict rules typed by hand. A small twist in how words are said can flip everything around. Swap just one term, watch the direction jump elsewhere - little things pull hard on results. Old-school checks stay fixed, repeat the same steps every time. Real conversations? They zigzag, backtrack, spin off suddenly. Meaning grows unevenly, never quite where you expect. Programs must keep up, step by step, no matter how wild the flow. One peek under the hood revealed word flow shapes outcomes across two models - Google’s Gemini 2.5 Flash, Meta’s LLaMA-3.3-70B. When speed played a role, Gemini pulled ahead each time. Success crowned most attempts, landing close to 96 percent done. Though LLaMA clocked past twenty seconds, Gemini frequently answered before ten elapsed. Trouble hit LLaMA in almost three out of ten tries, usually since replies ended mid-sentence. Still standing when pressure built, even if both began at the same pace. Close to a hundred trials unfolded - divided by how questions were framed and what kind of tasks were given. Clearer context gave a small boost - but differences didn’t close. One gliding ahead while the second stumbled again and again. Speed changes expose tiny delays that quietly add up. A shift becomes clear when studying these numbers. Closed tools resist the flow of real work rather than joining it. Once systems such as GPT-4 or Claude face deeper trials, perception could flip. What appears as one kind of code here may emerge unrecognizable there. Breaking major jobs into pieces leads to outcomes distant from rushed solo attempts.
ER  - 

