AB  - The advent of multi-agent systems powered by large language models has demonstrated significant promise for automated code generation, where collaborative crews of specialized agents produce clean and functional programs. However, the manual design of such optimal agent crews remains a complex challenge, requiring careful problem decomposition and agent role specification. To address this, we propose a grammar-based evolutionary algorithm (gbEA) to automate the design of multi-agent crews dedicated to code generation. We present a custom grammar for defining agent roles and task assignments, alongside tailored genetic operators and a fitness function, to enable the evolutionary generation of effective multi-agent systems. The proposed approach is evaluated on both a suite of mathematical problem-solving tasks and a code-generation benchmark; in all cases, the objective is to generate executable code. Experimental results indicate that the approach successfully synthesizes progressively more efficient agent crews capable of solving the target problems, demonstrating the viability of evolutionary algorithms (EAs) for optimizing collaborative AI-driven code generation systems.
ER  - 

