AB  - AI coding assistant tools (e.g., GitHub Copilot, Chat-GPT, and Cursor) have revolutionized the software engineering ecosystem. However, these tools are known to be highly vulnerable to data poisoning attacks, where adversaries intentionally inject malicious payloads into training datasets to manipulate the generated code. While the theoretical feasibility of these attacks is well-established, it remains unclear how the interface-level design of these tools influences developers’ susceptibility to adopting poisoned suggestions in practice. Building on our prior work, which demonstrated high developer susceptibility to poisoned models, we conducted an in-lab user study with 40 professional developers to systematically evaluate the impact of UI-level countermeasures. Participants completed realistic programming tasks using a poisoned chat-based AI coding assistant under three distinct interface conditions: a guided Skeleton UI, a Highlighted UI, and an explicit Warning UI, each compared against a baseline condition without any countermeasure. Our results reveal that the user interface is not merely a passive delivery mechanism, but an active layer of defense that influences security outcomes. We found that generic warnings were routinely dismissed due to developer habituation, and purely visual highlighting often led to ambiguous interpretations. In contrast, the Skeleton UI—which introduces intentional friction by requiring developers to explicitly resolve security-sensitive parameters—significantly disrupted uncritical copy-paste behaviors and reduced the incorporation of poisoned code. Ultimately, our findings demonstrate that mitigating the security risks of AI coding assistants requires going beyond upstream model defenses to incorporate security-aware, human-centric interface designs at the critical point of code adoption.
ER  - 

