AB  - Machine learning research is typically disseminated through academic papers that contain lengthy, complex multimodal content, including text, diagrams, and tabular results. Translating these papers into executable code repositories is a challenging and time-consuming process. When tackling paper reproduction tasks, existing multi-agent frameworks struggle with processing long and complex multimodal inputs from academic papers, often failing to effectively extract and prioritize implementation-relevant information from diverse content types. Additionally, these approaches lack detailed consideration of how to generate executable code repositories that maintain semantic consistency with the original paper content, resulting in implementations that may run successfully but deviate from the paper’s intended design and methodology. To address these challenges, we present AutoP2C, an innovative four-stage multi-agent framework that processes lengthy multimodal paper content and enhances semantic consistency between automatically generated code and paper content. Our framework systematically integrates external knowledge injection, paper content preprocessing, hierarchical task decomposition, and iterative feedback-driven implementation. Experiments on two datasets (including over 20+ ICML 2024 papers) demonstrate substantial improvements over existing baselines in both code quality and semantic alignment. The code is available at https://github.com/shoushouyu/AutomatedPaper-to-Code.
