AB  - A critical security risk is emerging at the intersection of autonomous vehicles (AVs), smart contracts, and large language models (LLMs). As AVs start acting as their own payment agents for services such as parking or tolls, they often need to interact with external smart contracts. In this context, a new and serious threat appears that is highly convincing smart contract honeypots created with the help of LLMs, which can be finetuned to insert subtle, hard-to-spot vulnerabilities into contracts that look completely legitimate. Such honeypots may target the deterministic decision-making process of an AV's payment system, allowing attackers to quietly drain the vehicle's digital wallet or gather sensitive information without raising immediate suspicion. This survey synthesizes existing research and highlights LLM-generated smart contract honeypots as an emerging threat to AV payment systems.
