AB  - Nontechnical users face a steep barrier when attempting to build web applications, since conventional development pipelines span programming, interface design, and systems integration domains that collectively demand years of specialized training. Although recent large language model-based code generation systems demonstrate strong syntactic fluency, existing approaches rely on direct prompt-to-code translation without structured reasoning, coordinated validation, or deployment-level architectural consistency, rendering them inadequate for production-grade software engineering tasks. To address these limitations, this work proposes Devonix, a hierarchical multi-agent neuro-symbolic orchestration framework for autonomous web application synthesis. The proposed architecture introduces Hierarchical Intent Modeling to decompose unstructured user requirements into verifiable subtask schemas, a GPU-accelerated LLM synthesis engine to generate modular HTML, CSS, and JavaScript artifacts, and a Smart Code Regeneration pipeline for adaptive repair of structural and logical defects. A Context-Aware Workflow Orchestration mechanism preserves session continuity across iterative refinements, while an Automated QA Validation Framework enforces syntactic correctness and runtime simulation compliance prior to deployment. Experiments conducted across 50 stratified web application generation tasks demonstrate that Devonix achieves an architectural accuracy of 96.8%, structural integrity of 94.2%, and a 63% reduction in functional errors, with an average deployment latency of 2.1 seconds, confirming significant gains over prompt-based and manual development baselines (p < 0.001).
