AB  - The proliferation of open-source large language models (LLMs) on platforms such as Hugging Face has introduced a structural selection challenge for enterprise software teams. Existing decision frameworks for enterprise LLM adoption are qualitative and manual, while comparative performance studies lack a systematic methodology for identifying which specific opensource model to deploy for a given production context. This paper proposes a ten-parameter, use-case-weighted evaluation framework that produces a quantified fit score from repository metadata alone, without requiring direct model inference. The license parameter operates as a binary hard filter. The remaining nine parameters are scored on an ordinal rubric and combined through use-case-specific weights into a composite Fit Score, classifying models as Strong Fit, Conditional Fit, or Not Recommended. A set of use-case-specific veto rules enforces capability floors that override the composite score, ensuring that structural deficits cannot be offset by strength in unrelated parameters. The ten parameters identified are defined across three groups: (i) Trustworthiness, (ii) Technical Fit, and (iii) Enterprise Compliance, with parameter weights differentiated across three targeted deployment use cases: (i) Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG), (ii) Code Generation, and (iii) Agentic Deployment. A model card completeness rubric with three classification tiers is introduced, including a use-case-aware reclassification rule that promotes benchmark documentation to a required component for Code Generation. The framework is demonstrated in a corpus that spans seven license types with parameter scales from 3.8B to 70B, producing meaningful, differentiated fit scores that reflect both usecase-specific capability requirements and enterprise compliance constraints, further identifying four conditions under which commercial models are preferable to open-source deployment. Since the framework scores repository metadata rather than model behaviour, fit scores express deployment readiness rather than measured task performance.
ER  - 

