AB  - LLM-based coding agents are increasingly used in interactive development workflows, but their automation boundary remains difficult to control from workflow data: a candidate action may be safe to execute automatically, may require more user intent, may need human review, or may need to be blocked. This paper studies automation-boundary control for intent-driven LLM coding workflows. We propose a data-driven hierarchical automation gate that separates candidate programming actions from control decisions and routes each action to AUTO, CLARIFY, REVIEW, or BLOCK. The gate uses operational signals for intent uncertainty, engineering risk, validation evidence, cross-layer consistency, and recent validation-based trust. The implemented policy is an interpretable threshold-based gate; the study does not optimize a POMDP or CMDP solver. The formulas below define the exact routing rules used in the reported experiments. We evaluate the gate on controlled public-data-derived states, 300 SWE-bench Lite-derived workflow instances with 900 gating states, three-group label agreement, deterministic baselines, ablations, parameter sweeps, and a small closed-loop repository audit. The results show that the proposed hierarchical gate reduces unsafe or premature automation while preserving automation for well-specified low-risk actions, providing a practical control layer for safer and more auditable LLM-based coding workflows.
ER  - 

