AB  - Recent advances in agentic AI have transformed software engineering by enabling autonomous reasoning, coding, and iterative problem solving. Applying these capabilities to hardware design presents new challenges, particularly in backend physical design, where timing closure remains one of the most time-consuming and least automated stages of the design flow. Rather than replacing mature electronic design automation (EDA) algorithms, agentic AI offers the opportunity to orchestrate existing CAD tools through closed-loop optimization, automating many of the decisions traditionally performed by experienced implementation engineers. The Agentic FPGA Backend Optimization Competition at FPL'26 invites teams to build autonomous agents that consume a fully placed-and-routed AMD UltraScale+™ design checkpoint and produce a functionally equivalent checkpoint with improved maximum clock frequency (Fmax). The contest combines agentic AI, the open-source RapidWright framework, and the commercial AMD Vivado™ toolchain, exposed through Model Context Protocol (MCP) servers, to lower the barrier to developing custom backend optimizations. This paper documents the competition, its benchmark suite, evaluation methodology, supporting infrastructure, and preliminary results. Nearly 100 academic and industry institutions, representing over 300 participants from 26 countries, are developing AI agents for backend physical design. By open-sourcing the benchmarks, infrastructure, and reference implementations, the contest aims to establish a reusable research platform for closed-loop, agentic backend optimization, enabling future work to be compared on common benchmarks and evaluation metrics. Final results will be documented at https://xilinx.github.io/fpl26_optimization_contest/.
ER  - 

