AB  - Autonomous LLM agents now routinely manipulate file systems and APIs in high-stakes software engineering workflows. While reactive frameworks like AgentSpec monitor individual actions, they fail to account for unsafe outcomes that emerge only from a cumulative sequence of individually compliant steps. This survey analyzes LLM safety across four methodological areas, identifying a fundamental disconnect between a single action enforcement and the reality of multi- stage exploits. We find that the field lacks not only proactive, sequence- level mechanisms for code agents but also the formal probabilistic models required to bound stochastic behavior and behavioral drift. Moreover, current benchmarks are shown to be predominantly static, failing to capture the complexity of real-world trajectories. By mapping these systemic gaps, we define the requirements for a proactive safety roadmap, emphasizing the transition toward dynamic, sequence-aware enforcement and multi-step evaluation metrics to secure agents in production environments.
