AB  - The rapid evolution of mobile platform complexity has revealed inherent deficiencies in conventional deterministic automation frameworks for software quality assurance. Script-based testing paradigms, which rely on hardcoded UI locators and predefined workflow sequences, impose a significant maintenance burden on engineering teams while failing to scale with the dynamism of modern application ecosystems. This article presents an industry-perspective synthesis, informed by an industrial deployment context, examining the architectural and organizational transition from static workflow automation to autonomous agentic systems, in which a large language model (LLM) serves as a reasoning engine connected to mobile environments through the Model Context Protocol (MCP). The work situates this transition within the broader landscape of LLM-based software engineering agents, including repository-level coding agents, multi-agent orchestration frameworks, and protocol-level tool integration standards, and positions mobile end-to-end testing as a distinctive case with its own constraints on latency, device heterogeneity, and continuous integration. Rather than presenting a controlled empirical study, the article discusses the mechanics of context engineering, multimodal state interpretation, and autonomous task planning as observed in practice, and evaluates the governance, security, and reliability challenges of granting autonomous execution authority to AI agents within continuous integration pipelines. The article closes with an assessment of the evolving role of the quality engineer and a research agenda outlining the controlled, statistically rigorous evaluation that this practitioner-oriented account does not itself attempt to provide.
ER  - 

