AB  - The proliferation of Large Language Models (LLMs) driving autonomous agents has introduced significant software engineering challenges to enterprise automation, particularly concerning the fragility, governance, and long-term maintainability of the services (tools) they invoke [3]. Current Model Context Protocol (MCP) standards are often basic. They lack necessary context, enumerable intents, and discoverability features. Consequently, they neglect the critical non-functional requirements essential for production-grade agent services [6]. This paper introduces the OpenMCPSpec Framework, a novel, extensible specification that formally embeds governance metadata, service lifecycle policies, and LLM reliability hints directly into the service contract [7]. We demonstrate the specification's structure through a formal schema and a Python-based reference implementation [9]. We propose validation metrics—Argument Correctness Improvement (ACI) and Compliance Pre-emption Rate (CPR)—to quantify the framework's effectiveness in enhancing LLM tool-calling reliability and maintaining enterprise compliance [15].
