AB  - Recent Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly integrated into the workflows of software engineers in large enterprises for generating code, for documenting software, for testing, for debugging, and for performing architecture design. These functionalities are typically integrated into single-agent systems which are not well suited for dealing with a variety of tasks, context, and dependencies. They thus lack support for end-to-end secure workflows. This paper proposes a Multi-Agent Generative AI Framework for Secure Enterprise Software Engineering that coordinates a set of domain-specific AI agents in a Task Orchestration Engine. The proposed framework includes, among others, agents for requirement analysis, for architecture design, for code generation, for security analysis, for testing, for compliance checking, for DevSecOps automation, and for enterprise knowledge management. A representative conceptual enterprise workflow that utilizes the proposed framework of agents is provided. A set of evaluation metrics to measure several aspects such as the completion of tasks, the coordination between agents, the time for completing workflows, the quality of communication, the degree of readiness of generated deployments for human-driven running, and the degree of human intervention in the automated processes of the framework are provided and qualitatively discussed. Such a system has clear potential for supporting collaborative software engineering, for securing end-to-end workflows, for using context inbetween different knowledge-reuse events, and for running enterprise-scale human-driven software releases in DevSecOps environments.
