AB  - Software defect prediction is crucial for enhancing software quality and efficient utilization of test resources. In this study, a novel and versatile approach for automated software defect prediction, code review, and code optimization is proposed. The approach incorporates three datasets: CodeXGLUE, Software Defect Prediction, and CodeSearchNet, along with data preprocessing procedures, AST feature extraction. A T5-based transformer is employed for code repair and review with the support of a rule-based fallback strategy, whereas a CPU-optimized T5/CodeGen model manages light-weight optimization. Ensemble models, including Gradient Boosting, XGBoost, Support Vector Machines, and Logistic Regression, are used for defect prediction tasks. The system is validated by measuring metrics including accuracy, precision, recall, F1, ROC-AUC, BLEU, CodeBLEU, similarity, change rate, syntax score, and SHAP values. Calibration and aggregation of agents is done via an optimized agent utilizing adaptive weighted scoring with nonlinear extensions to generate the final result. The experimental findings indicate that Gradient Boosting yields the highest accuracy (79.38%), XGBoost exhibits higher recall (54.39%), and the fused agent achieves a final value of 78.42. It is evident that the explainable and modular multi-agent design incorporates deep learning, transformer-based large language models, rule-based reasoning, and ensemble learning for accurate, interpretable, and scalable software defect detection and code optimization.
ER  - 

