AB  - This paper describes a project addressing a major gap in financial technology. A point of sale multi agent system. Its primary objective is to simultaneously address and resolve customers’ problems and inquiries. The project addresses several problem areas. Diagnosing hardware faults, retrieving product information and pricing and providing information about bank commissions. These are all handled within a single system giving rise to a project built on a multi agent architecture. Each response is based on the data obtained and the resulting answers do not deviate from this evidence. A graph based orchestrator classifies each incoming query through a schema constrained language model call and routes it to one of three specialists suited to its domain. Not all information is handled the same way. Unstructured regulatory guidelines go through semantic search. Operational data, such as the 61 node fault diagnosis tree is different. It is queried deterministically, so the same input always returns the same result. Both of these access paths, along with other tools, sit within a shared service layer that serves both internal agents and external clients through the Model Context Protocol. On a benchmark of 30 queries, the system reaches 93.3% routing accuracy. In a groundedness assessment carried out by a single annotator. 100% of the resulting responses were traceable to the retrieved evidence. Latency across agent types ranged from 1.9 to 5.8 seconds. These results directly pave the way for reliable artificial intelligence support systems in regulatory environments requiring compliance.
