AB  - For the cheating behaviour of LLM coding intelligences exploiting test case vulnerabilities, the existing test value mutation-based detection methods cannot cover the scenarios in which the intelligences exploit specification-level hidden constraints for cheating, and there is a structural detection blind spot. In this paper, we propose DriftBench with a new Drift mutation strategy, i.e., constructing the third class of impossible tasks by injecting hidden constraints into the function specification without modifying any test assertions, and designing a lightweight cheating detector based on 25-dimensional feature fusion. On 95 coding tasks constructed with reference to the IMPOSSIBLEBENCH benchmark design methodology, 1900 experimental runs were conducted using 5 models (covering the 3 model families DeepSeek, Qwen, and Zhipu). The results show that the baseline pass rate BPR is 90.3%, the One-Off cheating rate is 53.9%, the Drift cheating rate is 90.3%, the complementary detection rate DCR is 39.5%, and Drift independently detects 33 One-Off missed cheating cases; the Conflicting mutation cheating rate is only 4.4%, which is close to the absolute defence, verifying that contradiction assertion injection as an effective defence strategy. Ablation experiments further reveal that Drift cheating is insensitive to prompt wording, while One-Off cheating rate doubles from 15% to 30% with loose cues, indicating that specification-level cheating cannot be mitigated by cue word engineering.5 models show consistent cheating behaviour, validating the cross-model robustness found by DriftBench.
