AB  - Aiming at the dynamic coupling problem between encoding quality control and bit budget allocation in immersive media and three-dimensional audio-visual (3DAV) applications, this paper conducts a preliminary study on dynamic encoding resource allocation for 3DAV. We construct a single-stream, GOP-level prototype environment, and decouple the control task into a quality adjustment agent and a budget adjustment agent. Under the framework of centralized training and decentralized execution, multi-agent proximal policy optimization (MAPPO) is adopted to achieve cooperative optimization. Based on the YUV420p sequences of the UVG dataset, experiments are carried out at three target bitrates of 4500, 5500, and 7000 kbps via both simulated encoding and the real Kvazaar encoder, with fixed quantization parameter (QP) and heuristic QP adopted as baselines. The experimental results show that MAPPO achieves the best performance at the medium bitrate of 5500 kbps and outperforms the fixed QP baseline, while the heuristic QP maintains the optimal overall performance across all bitrate levels.This paper further supplements quantitative comparisons in terms of model complexity, deployment flexibility and generalization capability, and designs two lightweight 3DAV scenarios including 360° and multi-view proxy scenarios to validate the prototype scalability. The verification results demonstrate that multi-agent reinforcement learning is applicable to dynamic encoding resource allocation and presents competitive performance under medium budget constraints. Nevertheless, there remains considerable room for improvement in budget utilization accuracy, cross-bitrate adaptability, and support for practical 3DAV joint encoding.
ER  - 

