AB  - As cryptographic algorithms and protocols continue to evolve, extending and implementing open-source cryptographic suites has become increasingly complex. Traditional manual development is hindered by high entry barriers, strong framework dependencies, and lengthy verification cycles, making it difficult to support the efficient construction and continuous evolution of cryptographic software. Although Large Language Model (LLM)-based code generation has shown considerable promise in general-purpose software development, it remains insufficient for cryptographic engineering, where domain-specific knowledge, engineering constraints, and strict correctness and security requirements must be explicitly addressed. In this paper, we propose CryptForge, a knowledge-graph-enhanced multi-agent framework for automated cryptographic engineering. CryptForge adopts a decoupled dual-agent architecture consisting of a planning agent and a coding agent. The planning agent combines Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) with knowledge graph reasoning to enable accurate task decomposition and planning. The coding agent models the development workflow as a finite-state machine and performs state-aware code generation and iterative implementation through expert rules and context-aware retrieval. To further improve reliability, we introduce a sandbox-based Generate-Verify-Fix feedback loop that enhances compilability and correctness. We evaluate CryptForge on the SM4-HCTR integration task. The results show that CryptForge achieves a File Coverage Rate (FCR) of 80.95% and reduces the manual Code Modification Rate (CMR) to 9.91%, significantly outperforming existing general-purpose AI coding assistants.
ER  - 

