AB  - The proliferation of scientific data has intensified the demand for intuitive visualization tools that bridge the gap between complex data structures and human interpretability. While large language models (LLMs) have demonstrated promising capabilities in code generation for data visualization, existing approaches often suffer from semantic misalignment between natural language descriptions and visual encoding specifications, leading to inaccurate or misleading scientific charts. This paper proposes a novel VisAlign framework that explicitly aligns multimodal representations to enable precise natural language-driven scientific visualization generation. Our method introduces a cross-modal contrastive alignment module that synchronizes textual semantics with visual encoding priors, coupled with a hierarchical decoding pipeline that translates aligned representations into executable visualization code. Extensive experiments on MatPlotBench and Plot2Code benchmarks demonstrate that our approach achieves 72.4% accuracy in visualization generation, outperforming state-of-the-art LLM-based agents by 6.7 percentage points. The proposed framework significantly reduces hallucination errors in numerical representation and enhances the fidelity of generated scientific charts.
