AB  - Large language models (LLMs) are widely deployed for supporting interactive conversations, text summarization, and coding agents. These applications impose stringent latency requirements on computation, communication, and I/O, where every microsecond counts. In these environments, the CPU-driven GPU kernel launches become a significant concern. A CUDA Graph reduces this overhead by consolidating multiple kernel launches into a single graph replay. Even though widely adopted for LLM inference frameworks, its effectiveness has primarily been examined under single-node, general machine learning workloads. Consequently, its performance implications for distributed LLM inference across nodes remain largely underexplored. In this work, we systematically evaluate the benefit of CUDA graphs on distributed LLM inference. We analyze performance across various model sizes, parallelism strategies, and intra- and inter-node links, utilizing workloads that range from static ones (prefill and decode-dominant) to real-world traffic (BurstGPT and ServeGen traces).
