AB  - Dialogue troubleshooting agents can produce unsupported repair suggestions when the retrieval corpus lacks necessary evidence. We propose a constrained reinforcement-learning framework that trains an LLM-based agent to choose among three actions: retrieve, answer, or abstain. The abstain action returns a structured, grounded refusal that names the missing evidence instead of asking the user for clarification. We design a GRPO-style reward that balances task success, citation grounding, retrieval cost, over-refusal control, and clarification avoidance. We evaluate the method on TechQA, TopiOCQA, ASQA, and FaithDial using metrics for answer success, evidence coverage, hallucination on insufficient-evidence cases, and clarification rate. The reported numerical results are design-level screening results obtained with a Qwen2.5-style instruction backbone, QLoRA warm-up, a BM25-dense hybrid retriever, and a fixed top-k evidence budget; they should be interpreted as preliminary screening rather than final full-scale benchmark scores. Screening results show the agent substantially reduces unsupported answers while maintaining accuracy on sufficient-evidence cases.
