AB  - Multi-label clinical text classification is a core task in medical information processing, where clinical narratives are mapped to multiple diagnoses, procedures, phenotypes, risk factors, or disease categories. Although automated ICD coding remains the most mature benchmark setting, large language models (LLMs) are shifting the field from retrospective document-to-code mapping toward evidence-grounded, knowledge-augmented, and workflow-aware clinical prediction. This survey briefly positions pre-LLM label-aware and pretrained models as background, then focuses on LLM-era paradigms, including direct prompting, candidate verification, retrieval-augmented generation, rationale learning, and agentic coding workflows. We compare how these paradigms use LLMs as coders, verifiers, retrievers, explainers, or workflow agents, emphasizing their roles in label semantics, medical knowledge integration, and auditable prediction. We further summarize benchmarks, metrics, risks, and future directions across coding and diagnosis-oriented settings. Reliable clinical multi-labeling requires external knowledge, calibration, evidence grounding, abstention, and human oversight.
